\paragraph{Differential nocuity and intervention limits.}
Our results support a \emph{differential nocuity} hypothesis \citep{berry_kamsties_2004,willis_nocuous_2008}: the binary effect is domain-robust ($p = 0.187$) but type-specific profiles require per-domain calibration: cross-domain type ranking is near chance ($\rho = 0.26$, $p = 0.62$), driven by conditional precedence ($\Delta = 22.0$\,pp retail vs.\ airline); deployment in other domains requires independent type-effect calibration. Of the 60.8\% unexplained variance, $\sim$15\% is stochastic and $\sim$46\% systematic (OpenAI-family estimate; ICC $= 0.749$; Appendix~\ref{app:residual}). ICC reflects within-provider reproducibility; cross-provider ICC may be lower, but C1 directional consistency across all seven models from six providers offers complementary aggregate-level evidence. The 60.8\% residual also reflects a limitation of the current explanatory framework---unmeasured factors such as conversation trajectory, user-simulator behavior, and topic-specific interactions. Even perfect disambiguation would address at most 22.4\% of variance; prompt-based mitigation is type-dependent (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}), reinforcing policy-design over prompt-level fixes.

\paragraph{Taxonomy as diagnostic tool.}
Type membership explains only 0.4\% of violation variance versus 22.4\% for clause wording; however, it predicts failure \emph{mechanism} substantially more strongly than violation rate (Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.45$ vs.\ $0.14$, a 3.3$\times$ ratio; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}), validating the taxonomy as a diagnostic tool (\S\ref{sec:analysis}).

\paragraph{Ecological validity.}
Our synthetic design establishes causal mechanisms but overestimates naturalistic effect sizes. The SOPBench replication yields $+$12.6\,pp (three of five models significant; Appendix~\ref{app:sopbench}), 63\% smaller than the $\tau^2$-bench effect. Three structural differences---measurement grain (constraint-level vs.\ episode-level), baseline compression (22.9\% vs.\ 6.9\% unambiguous rate), and interaction depth (single-turn vs.\ multi-turn)---each independently predict attenuation, but their individual contributions cannot be isolated. Even at 12.6\,pp, the effect exceeds measurement noise ($>$2$\times$ perturbation control upper CI) and has practical significance for safety-critical deployments.

